# Repository-wide merge behaviour (#5373).
#
# Measured 2026-09-26: of 130 open PRs, 44 did not merge cleanly, and 33 of those
# conflicted on just three artifacts — 25 on `config/ci-surfaces.yml`, 8 on
# `docs/product/sdk-rename-table.md`, 4 on `config/surface-manifest.yml`. The two
# registries are APPEND-ONLY: two lanes registering a new entry land on the same
# anchor, and git reports a conflict on lines that do not disagree.
#
# `merge=union` is a git BUILT-IN — it needs NO `git config` in any clone, so this
# file alone is the whole setup. It keeps BOTH sides' lines for the conflicting
# hunk. That is right for an append-only registry, and WRONG for anything a YAML
# loader reads: union can emit a duplicate mapping key or a duplicate entry, and
# `yaml.safe_load` silently keeps the last one.
#
# So each unioned file is paired with a fail-closed validator that reddens the
# required `manifest-integrity` job (see `python-ci-gate`'s `needs`):
#   config/ci-surfaces.yml      -> tools/registry_integrity.py
#   config/surface-manifest.yml -> tools/registry_integrity.py
# Adding a unioned file here without adding a validator is a red test
# (tests/test_registry_integrity.py::test_validated_set_equals_unioned_set).
config/ci-surfaces.yml       merge=union
config/surface-manifest.yml  merge=union

# NOT unioned, deliberately — generated artifacts.
#
# `docs/product/sdk-rename-table.md` is a function of `sdk.py` line numbers. Both
# sides of a merge regenerate it against a different tree, so a line-level union
# duplicates rows and produces a table matching NEITHER side. Its conflict class is
# removed by not committing it (see `.gitignore`, #5373) and generating it on
# demand with `tools/sdk_rename_table.py` — not by unioning it, and not by a custom
# merge driver (a driver would have to regenerate from the MERGED `sdk.py`, which
# does not exist while a per-file driver runs, so it would be wrong by construction).
#
# Real source (`tortoise/sdk.py`, `tortoise/hosted_api.py`) is likewise NOT unioned:
# those are genuine semantic conflicts, and per-PR resolution is correct for them.
